WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.273
Gravitational-wave inference usually assumes
detector noise is Gaussian and stationary,

00:00:06.353 --> 00:00:07.685
but real LIGO, Virgo,

00:00:07.765 --> 00:00:12.260
and KAGRA data break that with glitches and
drifting spectral lines.

00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:16.767
This work introduces SLIC, Score-based Likelihood
Characterization.

00:00:16.847 --> 00:00:23.157
It learns the true noise distribution with a
diffusion model and couples it to a differentiable

00:00:23.237 --> 00:00:26.050
waveform for unbiased parameter estimation.

00:00:26.430 --> 00:00:31.748
Extracting astrophysics from gravitational waves
needs Bayesian inference,

00:00:31.828 --> 00:00:33.791
and that needs a likelihood.

00:00:33.871 --> 00:00:41.159
For decades the field assumed noise is Gaussian and
stationary because it makes the likelihood cheap.

00:00:41.239 --> 00:00:47.943
But real detectors drift over time and carry
transient glitches and wandering spectral lines.

00:00:48.023 --> 00:00:54.071
These are handled segment by segment with costly
custom fixes that can bias results.

00:00:54.151 --> 00:00:58.010
Famously, GW170817 landed right on a loud LIGO glitch.

00:00:58.390 --> 00:01:02.627
Why now?

00:01:02.707 --> 00:01:06.943
Gravitational-wave astronomy is scaling fast,

00:01:07.023 --> 00:01:11.260
and analyzing large catalogs makes the pipeline
sensitive to tiny departures from Gaussian

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:15.577
noise that quietly accumulate into bias.

00:01:15.657 --> 00:01:19.893
The authors wanted to keep the trusted
deterministic waveform models while dropping the unrealistic noise assumption,

00:01:19.973 --> 00:01:24.210
and to prepare for next-generation detectors like
Cosmic Explorer and the Einstein Telescope.

00:01:24.590 --> 00:01:29.696
The core contribution brings Score-based Likelihood
Characterization, or SLIC,

00:01:29.776 --> 00:01:31.226
to gravitational waves.

00:01:31.306 --> 00:01:34.284
Originally developed for astronomical imaging,

00:01:34.364 --> 00:01:40.801
it learns the score of the noise distribution with
a diffusion model instead of assuming its form.

00:01:40.881 --> 00:01:45.655
Coupled with a differentiable waveform, it yields
an unbiased likelihood.

00:01:45.735 --> 00:01:52.570
They show it both generates realistic LIGO noise
and recovers the true parameters of an injected signal.

00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:54.400
Here is how SLIC works.

00:01:54.480 --> 00:02:02.185
The setting is an inverse problem with additive
noise: an observation equals a deterministic signal model plus noise.

00:02:02.265 --> 00:02:08.040
Learn the score of the noise distribution and you
can build the score of the likelihood.

00:02:08.120 --> 00:02:14.294
So they train a score-based diffusion model on real
LIGO noise using denoising score matching,

00:02:14.374 --> 00:02:20.681
then chain it with the Jacobian of a differentiable
IMRPhenomD waveform from the ripple package.

00:02:20.761 --> 00:02:24.740
Sampling uses a Metropolis-adjusted Langevin
algorithm, MALA.

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:29.394
The demonstration uses real data.

00:02:29.474 --> 00:02:33.749
They train the score network on eleven hours of
real LIGO-Hanford data around GW150914,

00:02:33.829 --> 00:02:38.103
sampled at four thousand ninety-six hertz in
four-second segments,

00:02:38.183 --> 00:02:42.457
discarding the segment holding the true signal.

00:02:42.537 --> 00:02:46.811
Training runs in the Fourier domain after a Tukey window.

00:02:46.891 --> 00:02:51.166
To test,

00:02:51.246 --> 00:02:55.520
they inject a simulated GW150914-like signal into
held-out real noise never seen during training.

00:02:55.900 --> 00:02:57.680
The results are encouraging.

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:01.800
Drawing one thousand twenty-four noise realizations
from SLIC,

00:03:01.880 --> 00:03:06.052
its power spectrum matches a Welch estimate from
real LIGO data,

00:03:06.132 --> 00:03:11.634
and it resolves narrow spectral lines even more
finely thanks to more training data.

00:03:11.714 --> 00:03:13.295
Injecting a known signal,

00:03:13.375 --> 00:03:17.481
SLIC recovers all five varied parameters with high
credibility,

00:03:17.561 --> 00:03:21.600
and its posterior is tighter than the
Gaussian-likelihood one.

00:03:21.980 --> 00:03:25.548
The key comparison is against the conventional approach.

00:03:25.628 --> 00:03:31.281
For the injected signal they run inference twice:
once with the learned SLIC likelihood,

00:03:31.361 --> 00:03:35.646
and once with a standard Gaussian likelihood from a
Welch spectrum.

00:03:35.726 --> 00:03:40.142
They pick a glitch-free segment, so both recover
the true parameters,

00:03:40.222 --> 00:03:44.441
but SLIC's posterior comes out tighter, hinting at
gains on noisy,

00:03:44.521 --> 00:03:46.070
glitch-contaminated data.

00:03:46.450 --> 00:03:49.166
A few numbers anchor the demonstration.

00:03:49.246 --> 00:03:53.610
Eleven hours of real LIGO-Hanford noise train the
score model.

00:03:53.690 --> 00:03:59.631
One thousand twenty-four synthetic realizations
validate the learned power spectrum.

00:03:59.711 --> 00:04:04.362
Inference recovers five source parameters: chirp
mass, mass ratio,

00:04:04.442 --> 00:04:07.874
luminosity distance, coalescence time, and phase.

00:04:07.954 --> 00:04:13.250
Data are sampled at four thousand ninety-six hertz
in four-second segments.

00:04:13.630 --> 00:04:18.206
The takeaway: you no longer have to assume
Gaussian, stationary noise.

00:04:18.286 --> 00:04:25.390
By learning the real noise distribution with a
diffusion model and coupling it to a differentiable waveform,

00:04:25.470 --> 00:04:29.714
SLIC produces an unbiased likelihood straight from
detector data.

00:04:29.794 --> 00:04:34.637
This proof of concept, currently single-detector
and four-second segments,

00:04:34.717 --> 00:04:36.566
points toward fast, scalable,

00:04:36.646 --> 00:04:40.690
unbiased inference for future gravitational-wave
observations.
